{"name":"Northern Sky","hex":"#8daccc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"northern","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, facing, situated in, or related to the north; northerly.","Blowing from the north; northerly."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inhabitant of the northern regions; a northerner.","The northern pike."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/northern"},{"term":"sky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sikaiana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The atmosphere above a given point, especially as visible from the surface of the Earth as the place where the sun, moon, stars, and clouds are seen.","With a descriptive word: the part of the sky which can be seen from a specific place or at a specific time; its climate, condition, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Northern Sky","blocks":[{"text":"\"Northern Sky\" is a song from the English singer-songwriter Nick Drake's 1971 album Bryter Layter, produced by Joe Boyd. During the recording sessions for the album, the chronically shy and withdrawn songwriter formed a friendship and a mentorship of sorts with producer Joe Boyd, an early supporter of Drake. Boyd saw commercial potential in the acoustic and unaccompanied demo version of the song, and recruited former Velvet Underground member John Cale as producer. Cale added piano, organ and celesta arrangements, initially against Drake's wishes."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e0/Nick_Drake_Northern_Sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Sky","wikidata":"Q7058965"},{"title":"Northern celestial hemisphere","blocks":[{"text":"The northern celestial hemisphere, also called the Northern Sky, is the northern half of the celestial sphere; that is, it lies north of the celestial equator. This arbitrary sphere appears to rotate westward around a polar axis due to Earth's rotation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/North_Hemisphere.png/330px-North_Hemisphere.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_celestial_hemisphere","wikidata":"Q1998069"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky","wikidata":"Q527"},{"title":"Sky blue","blocks":[{"text":"Sky blue refers to a collection of shades comparable to that of a clear daytime sky. Typically it is a shade of cyan or light teal, though some iterations are closer to light azure or light blue. The term is attested from 1681. A 1585 translation of Nicolas de Nicolay's 1576 Les navigations, peregrinations et voyages faicts en la Turquie includes \"the tulbant [turban] of the merchant must be skie coloured\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Shades_of_light_blue.png/330px-Shades_of_light_blue.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_blue","wikidata":"Q373160"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg/500px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg/1280px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg/500px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1515 map of the northern celestial hemisphere by Albrecht Dürer. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlbrecht_D%C3%BCrer%2C_The_Northern_Celestial_Hemisphere%2C_1515%2C_NGA_43181.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg/500px-Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg/1280px-Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg/500px-Sky_over_Munich_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cirriform clouds and a quarter Moon in the blue daytime sky over Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASky_over_Munich_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bangladesh.jpg/500px-Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bangladesh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bangladesh.jpg/1280px-Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bangladesh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bangladesh.jpg/500px-Blue_Sky%2C_Dhaka%2C_Bang